       On Linux, a message queue descriptor is  actually  a  file  descriptor.
       (POSIX  does  not  require  such an implementation.)  This means that a
       message queue descriptor can be monitored using sseelleecctt(2), ppoollll(2),  or
       eeppoollll(7).  This is not portable.

       The  close‐on‐exec  flag (see ooppeenn(2)) is automatically set on the file
       descriptor returned by mmqq__ooppeenn(2).

